---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { tools } from '../data/tools.json';

// Cheapest paid entry point first; free-to-start tools sort by what the first
// paid tier costs, because that is the number people actually compare.
const rows = [...tools].sort((a, b) => a.priceFrom - b.priceFrom);

const cheapestPaid = rows.filter(t => t.priceFrom > 0)[0];
const freeToStart = rows.filter(t => t.freeTier);
const lovable = tools.find(t => t.id === 'lovable');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AI App Builder Pricing Compared"
  description="What every major AI app builder actually costs: entry price, free tier, and who each one is worth paying for. Lovable, Cursor, Bolt, Bubble, Webflow and more."
>
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">
      What these tools actually cost
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg mb-8" style="color: var(--color-muted);">
      Entry prices for every builder we've reviewed, side by side. Vendors change
      pricing often, so treat this as a starting point and check the current page
      before you pay.
    </p>

    <div class="disclosure-notice">
      <span class="font-bold shrink-0 mt-0.5" style="color: var(--color-accent);">ℹ</span>
      <span>
        Some links here are affiliate links. They never change the order of this
        table, which is sorted by price.&#32;
        <a href="/disclosure" class="hover:underline ml-1" style="color: var(--color-accent-light);">Full disclosure →</a>
      </span>
    </div>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl" style="border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
      <table class="w-full text-sm" style="border-collapse: collapse;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-2);">
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">Tool</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-text);">Starts at</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-text);">Free tier</th>
            <th class="text-left font-semibold px-4 py-3" style="color: var(--color-text);">Worth paying for if</th>
            {/* relative: sr-only is absolutely positioned, and without a
                positioned ancestor it escapes the scroll container and widens
                the whole document on mobile. */}
            <th class="px-4 py-3 relative"><span class="sr-only">Links</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(tool => (
            <tr style="border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top">
                <div class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--color-text);">{tool.name}</div>
                <a href={`/blog/${tool.reviewSlug}`} class="link-accent text-xs">Read the review</a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top whitespace-nowrap" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.priceFrom === 0 ? 'Free tier only' : `$${tool.priceFrom}/mo`}
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.freeTier ? 'Yes' : 'No'}
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top" style="color: var(--color-prose-body);">
                {tool.bestFor}
                <div class="text-xs mt-1" style="color: var(--color-muted);">Skip it for: {tool.notFor}</div>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top">
                <a
                  href={`/go/${tool.id}`}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="nofollow sponsored noopener"
                  data-tool={tool.id}
                  class="btn-primary py-1.5 px-3 text-xs whitespace-nowrap"
                >
                  Try it →
                </a>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Guidance -->
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none mt-12">
      <h2>How to read this table</h2>

      <p>
        The entry price is the least useful number on it. Every one of these
        tools bills against something that runs out, whether that's messages,
        credits, or compute minutes, and the tier you actually end up on depends
        on how much you build rather than which plan you picked on day one.
      </p>

      <p>
        {freeToStart.length} of these {rows.length} let you start without a card.
        Use that. Build the same small thing on two of them and you'll learn more
        in an afternoon than any comparison table can tell you, including this one.
      </p>

      <h2>If you're picking one to pay for</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>You can already code.</strong> Cursor or GitHub Copilot. The
        cheapest paid option in the table is {cheapestPaid.name} at ${cheapestPaid.priceFrom}/mo,
        and it's the right first step if you mostly want faster autocomplete
        rather than an agent rewriting files. Cursor costs twice that and earns
        it once you're doing multi-file work.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You can't, and you want a real app.</strong> Lovable, at
        ${lovable.priceFrom}/mo and the only tool here with no free tier. It costs
        more than an editor because it hands you a database and auth without you
        understanding either. That's the thing you're actually buying, and it's
        also the thing that will bite you the first time something breaks.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You want a site, not an app.</strong> Framer or Webflow. Framer
        gets you there faster, Webflow gives you more room to grow. Neither one
        needs the AI builders at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>You're not sure yet.</strong> Don't pay anyone. Start on Bolt's
        free tier, get something ugly working, and let that tell you which of the
        problems above you actually have.
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>
